task: lexer-state-allocation
agent: opus5-lex
started: 2026-09-09
branch: feature/lexer-state-allocation
worktree: ../okay-wt-lexstate
spec: docs/benchmarks.md §10 — BACKLOG "lexer-state-allocation"

why: measured by chunked-lexer-bookkeeping, load-proof: lexing
  allocates ~180 B per input CHARACTER on BOTH paths (453 KB
  element-wise for a 2 495-char document). `okay.lex.Json`'s state is
  `S(mode, buf: String, start: P, cur: P)` and every `step` does
  `s.copy(buf = s.buf + c, cur = s.cur + c)` — a fresh String per
  character, QUADRATIC in the token's length, plus a new S, a new P
  and the Tuple2 `step` answers.

what: the token's text carried as a START OFFSET, sliced from the
  input ONCE at `finish`, instead of a String grown per character.
  No interface change (`Scan.step` keeps its shape — that is
  `scan-step-allocation`, a separate item measured after this one).

the hazard to solve, not to ignore: the CHUNKED path never has the
  whole input — `Lex.chunks` sees one chunk at a time and a token can
  span chunks, so an offset alone cannot be sliced there. Whatever
  shape the state takes must serve both paths, and the chunked one is
  the constraint. If it cannot, say so with the measurement and stop.

gate: B/op first (target: the ~180 B/char), on the Writer-only lex
  lanes; a quiet box for time; lands only if the numbers show it AND
  the lex/parse/codec suites are green — token identity, spans and
  the incremental relex laws are the contract, not speed.
